the new aircraft carrier hms queen  elizabeth is to lead a strike force of  ships and planes around the world next  month  setting off from portsmouth in the uk's  largest signal of maritime and air power  in a generation let's speak now to  defence secretary ben wallace who's in  portsmouth  this morning good morning to you um i  want to come on to the  deployment in a moment but first of all  can i ask you about india  and what the the specific aid is that  that we're sending due to arrive  tomorrow yes  the united kingdom is going to send to  india oxygen compressors  and ventilators things that are are  really needed in the now as we've seen  from your own reports the pressure on  hospitals  in india is getting unbearable and you  know we're going to do our part to make  sure our friends  india get all the support they can we  obviously if you remember commissioned  huge numbers of ventilators to  alleviate the pressure on the hospitals  and it's only right that we share and  help them at their time in need  do you wish that the decision had been  taken sooner to provide this this aid  we know that they're sending 600 pieces  of medical equipment 495  oxygen uh concentrators but thousands  are  dying every day and we know that they're  dying in record numbers  well look this government alongside the  united states with the indian government  and  responding to their request that that's  the key to it is is that  it isn't like we just woke up this  morning and thought we'd do it it's been  properly worked out and planned with the  indian government  and made sure that it became available  as soon as soon as needed and you know  if necessary we'll put it in military  planes to get it there or charter other  planes  we'll do everything we can to make sure  we alleviate that suffering  will we be foregoing our vaccines from  india in order to to boost  their program and and will we be sending  vaccines and and test kits  well now first of all as your reports  show that the pressure at the moment  is in the hospital system and that's  where we're focusing our support and our  effort to make sure  that they help get through that on  vaccines obviously india itself is  manufacturing huge amounts of vaccines  it's got  millions and millions of doses and will  obviously be able to  roll itself through that program but  just if you remember if your viewers  cast their mind back  to our outbreaks and both the time of  the second and the first lockdown  this was about pressure on the nhs and  making sure that we the pipeline of  patients going into it  was managed because once the nhs or any  hospital system  gets overloaded you see what you see in  india and that's why we're helping  at that critical point in the process  both with the lessons that we have  learned but also with equipment that we  are no longer or not using or the  surplus  and sending it over to them back here  where we're reading this morning about  boris johnson allegedly saying that he'd  rather  see bodies pile higher in their  thousands than order a third  lockdown were you aware of of any such  comment and if  true i mean it's it's really shocking  isn't it but i i think i think look look  it's not true it's been categorically  denied by practically everyone  uh you know we're getting into the sort  of comedy chapter now of these gossip  and uh  stories and unnamed sources by unnamed  advisors talking about  unnamed events you know look i don't  none of this is is serious the prime  minister and as your viewers will know  has has been utterly focused on  delivering alongside cabinet colleagues  the response to covid whether that's  managing the hospitals and you know you  only have to look as you've just said  earlier  to what happens if that management gets  out of kilter in other countries and  then rolling out  the vaccine and you know today is really  good news over 62 percent of adults  in this country have a vaccine 62  over half the population have been  vaccinated we're way ahead of our  our contemporaries around the world and  that is a government-led rollout the  prime minister has been personally  engaged in it  and you know all the who said what said  what said you know i'll leave that to  the  oscar gossip columns that are now being  rolled out today after the night i'll  leave that to the hello magazines of the  world  uh but you know government is focused on  delivering for the viewer and the  citizen  a covered response you say that we're  getting into uh comedy territory and and  gossip columns but  uh i'll remind you of uh johnny mercer's  comments this is the veterans minister  uh who resigned uh some say sacked uh he  says that uh it's been the most awful  and distrustful environment to work in  uh almost nobody tells the truth um and  it's a cesspit  well uh johnny worked with me for uh  nearly two years in the department  you know he was a valued colleague uh  you know johnny has now left the  government he contributed to  to some of the policies towards helping  veterans he's campaigned  for veterans uh you know i campaigned  for my first veterans in 1998 so uh  he joins a large team of people doing  that campaign i i don't agree with any  of his observations about government  you know government is what you make of  it i i did every rank in government  before i became in the cabinet i was a  backbencher all the way through  the trick of being in government is  making sure that you focus and you  deliver  johnny did some really good work but you  know some of his comments i just don't  agree with  uh he does say that the government has  abandoned soldiers who served  in in the troubles he's actually in  belfast today  at a a trial um many would say  that he as a a veteran himself speaks  adequately  to that cause um what what do you say to  that criticism the government has  abandoned  these veterans well i think that's  wrong first of all the bill that johnny  started taking through parliament  is hopefully just about to complete  parliament this week another bill that  he started the armed forces bill and  which we  put in place in the law the armed forces  covenant the first government ever to  put that  covenant into the law or all the things  that he wanted that is  in process right now the simple fact is  if you want to help  veterans and you actually want to  deliver rather than just talk you need  to be in government  he was in government he did a brilliant  job we've got another new veterans  minister who's also a veteran serves in  afghanistan  was in the infantry he'll do a fantastic  job but fundamentally government does  the delivery  we've done that for overseas bills  that's the bill at the moment that will  protect  uh in future overseas deployments when  it comes to northern ireland veterans  i'm a northern ireland veteran i lost  soldiers in northern ireland  my soldiers some of them in similar  tours were convicted or put on trial for  events  i know exactly what it's like i know  right to the heart that community  we are committed to doing something for  them the government has said it i'm  convinced they are  and the northern ireland office  presented to johnny mercer and i only a  few weeks ago  some of their plans so we haven't  abandoned them we're going to deliver  and we're going to make sure we look  after our veterans  so you don't see it as embarrassing at  all that he's taking it upon himself  to be in belfast this morning to support  two veterans or on trial  i think it's embarrassing he's doing the  right thing in his eye i mean it's the  right thing to stand by people i don't  you know members of parliament regularly  attend and support their constituents  all their causes  he's doing what all member of parliament  does he's he's a he's a he's a very  doubty advocate of their cause you know  i'm a government minister i can't go and  prejudice a trial that would be  wholly wrong and i can't actually  comment on this trial and i think we  have to be  very very careful when there is ongoing  judicial proceedings as you yourself  will know  but you know look uh what is really key  and johnny has said this many many times  that uh you know if people break the law  no one is above the law in this country  whether they are soldiers or citizens no  one is that's the difference between us  and terrorists that's the difference  between us and some of those regimes  around the world  is we are not above the law but it is  perfectly right to make sure that when  people are investigated they're  investigated thoroughly  but not repeatedly and also when people  are sued by lawyers  who are seeking the money like phil  shiner if you remember from public  interest money  rather than actually seeking uh what  they would view as justice it's really  right we make steps to prevent that  happening that's what we're doing  at the moment johnny is continuing his  campaign and i i wish him all the very  best  uh let's talk about your announcement uh  today uh the carrier going to the  indo-pacific  region tell us about that  well i'm standing in front of a 65 000  ton ship  made in six united kingdom shipyards  with amazing skills  going into it it's it's the first fifth  generation  aircraft carrier of its type in nato  it's going to travel 26 000 miles to  japan  and back it's going to carry 3 000 plus  people  aircraft partly made in lancashire in my  own part of the world  and it is about projecting global  britain it's about saying  first of all that britain has this  capability back we all remember the ark  royal and  all those other aircraft carriers we had  it's about our place in the world  and making sure we're an anchor in nato  providing protection from  aggressive actions by our adversaries  and it's also about going across the  world to uphold things like the  international  rule of the sea the un convention of of  the sea and making sure that our allies  globally know that we're there in spirit  uh and  in physical power should they need it uh  one of the areas heading to  obviously will be china is it  appropriate to be  heading to china at this time  well we're heading to japan and we will  obviously transit through  the likes of the south china sea and  getting there but you know britain's  deployment is not to be provocative  britain's deployment is to go and  show show common value and uphold those  laws  that are sometimes threatened by a whole  range of people terrorists and other  uh sometimes more aggressive nations and  to go out there around the world  and show solidarity with australia and  new zealand but also to celebrate  something that most people don't really  know about but 50 years  this year ago 50 years ago the five  power  defense agreement came into being that  was new zealand australia malaysia  singapore and the united kingdom it's  the oldest  defense uh cooperation agreement uh in  the pacific and we're going over there  to celebrate that as well because that's  really really important that's long  before the current challenges and  instability  it goes right back and uh you know  that's why this ship is really important  she  she's you know she we should be proud of  her she's british and  and i'm proud that what we're going to  stand for is all those values of freedom  and the rule of law  but minister is it is it about a show of  power though  it's about lots of things so this ship  does everything it is it's not just a  hard military  piece of equipment it's a it's a  national convener we'll have united  states  aircraft on board will be accompanied by  a dutch frigate around the world we're  going to meet up with the french  charles de gaulle carrier in the  mediterranean it's a great convener  it's also a great show of british  capability and engineering and when we  go to places like  india and other countries on the route  it'll be about saying you know this is  what britain can do  this is what we can do together we'll  convene people along trade we'll convene  people in culture we'll convene decision  makers and all those countries to make  sure that you know britain  and those countries recognize that the  most important thing we have that some  of our adversaries don't have  we have alliances we have friends and we  have a mutual  uh belief in the values of democracy and  the rule of law and that's what we're  there  to do having a carrier is a really  important part of a tier one military  power  uh we we haven't had it for a few years  and i'm incredibly proud that it's now  ready to go and it will be setting sail  soon  next month ben wallace secretary of  state for defence thank you

